    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go a bit deeper into the intracellular signaling? Specifically, how does the presence of amino acids—not just leucine, but the general pool—interact with the Rag GTPases to actually shuttle mTORC1 to the lysosomal surface? I want to make sure I can explain the mechanism clearly to my client if they ask.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The presence of amino acids, particularly leucine, triggers the activation of Rheb (a GTP-binding protein) and its subsequent interaction with the Rag GTPase complex. This interaction activates the Rag C and G multifunctional protein (MFP), which then interacts with TORC1 (mTOR complex 1) to release its inhibitory lid. The released TORC1 then translocates to the lysosomal surface, where it becomes activated by the presence of amino acids. This activation enables TORC1 to translocate phosphorylation events downstream, ultimately stimulating protein synthesis and anabolic processes, including muscle growth. In essence, the general pool of amino acids helps engage mTORC1 by modulating its interaction with Rheb and Rag GTPases, thereby bypassing the other inhibitory pathways.",
      "source": "target",
